### Editing on the page, or in forms

TinaCMS shows the site and the editor side by side. The page updates in real time in React, and a click on an element opens its form. This works in Astro too, but each page is wired by hand: the `tina()` integration, `requestWithMetadata()` loaders, an island registry and route, `TinaIsland` and `tinaField()` in components. It suits editors who think in pages.

In Mica, the form is where editors work. The real Astro page sits beside it, with the drafts in it, about 1-2 seconds after typing stops. A click on the page moves the cursor to the field. Mica edits no text on the page itself, and it has no page builder. The templates need one integration, `micaPreview()`, and no other change. It suits editors who think in fields, and teams that do not want to touch each template.

### How Astro reads the content, and what is indexed

In Mica, the files in Git reach Astro as Content Collections, with types. The Tina guide for Astro uses a generated GraphQL client instead, not Content Collections. In both, the files stay the source of truth.

TinaCMS adds a Data Layer: a GraphQL API backed by a database that its documentation calls an ephemeral cache of your files. TinaCloud hosts it, or you run MongoDB or Redis/Vercel KV. A content index is not by itself a runtime dependency of the website. The Tina documentation does not say whether a published Astro site queries the index, so check how you build your pages. Mica has no index and no database in the editor, and the published website never calls Mica.

### Hosting, Git hosts and review

TinaCloud runs the backend for you, with plans and search. Self-hosted TinaCMS needs a backend function, a database adapter, a Git provider and an auth provider. The Editorial Workflow (Business plan and up) puts edits on a branch with a draft pull request.

Mica has no hosted offering. It runs as a local editor, or as one container that you run, with OpenID Connect sign-in and no database. It reads and writes a Git clone, so any Git host can work. Forgejo is tested in CI, and github.com and gitlab.com are in use. Mica has no review step in the editor: a team can review with a staging branch and a second Mica.

### Equal, or not documented

- Both keep content as files in Git, use a TypeScript model and have an official Astro package.
- Content in a Git submodule: the TinaCMS documentation does not say.
- Entries that link to a JSON or CSV file of reference data: the TinaCMS documentation does not say.
- Git LFS: the TinaCMS documentation does not say. It names limits of the GitHub API.
- Localization: TinaCMS documents two patterns, by directory or by field, and no locale switcher. In Mica, a field has a value for each language, and the editor has a language switcher.
